13:18:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right that I called the stats "fuzzy" earlier, so let's actually dig into whether that fuzziness supports "way higher than 10-15%" or not — because I think there's an important difference between "the exact number is uncertain" and "therefore your personal sample is a better estimate." ## The Fuzziness Was About Precision, Not Direction When I said the stats were fuzzy, I meant things like: different studies get 8% vs 15% vs 20% depending on methodology, diagnostic era, sample source, etc. That's uncertainty *within* a range that's still clearly "minority, not majority." None of the fuzziness in the actual research pushes the number anywhere close to 50%+ — nobody's serious estimate has ever landed there. So "fuzzy" doesn't mean "anything goes." ## Why Personal Encounters Are a Uniquely Bad Sample for This Specific Question This is the important part: the people you've "encountered" with Tourette's are almost certainly not a random sample — they're a **specifically biased sample**, for a structural reason: - People with **mild tics** (blinking, throat-clearing, small vocal sounds) often go **undiagnosed entirely**, or are diagnosed but never disclose it, because the symptoms aren't disruptive enough to prompt a diagnosis or a conversation about it. - People with **coprolalia** are, almost by definition, the ones you'd *notice* and *identify* as "having Tourette's" — because the symptom is loud, disruptive, and unmistakable. - So your personal sample isn't "people with Tourette's" — it's "people with Tourette's whose symptoms were severe/visible enough that you became aware they had it." That's a **selection filter that specifically inflates coprolalia's apparent rate**, by definition. This is actually a textbook example of **selection bias** — similar to "I've never met anyone who died of a heart attack in their 30s, so heart attacks must be rare in your 30s" — except heart attacks in someone's 30s that don't kill them don't get reported to you either, skewing what you're aware of. ## A Cleaner Way to See the Problem Ask yourself: **how would you even know if someone had Tourette's with only simple motor tics, if they never mentioned it and their tics were mild enough to blend into normal fidgeting/coughing/blinking?** You wouldn't. Meanwhile, someone with coprolalia is essentially guaranteed to be identified and remembered as "that person with Tourette's," because the symptom is impossible to miss. So the pool of "people I've personally encountered and identified as having TS" is going to systematically overrepresent dramatic/visible symptoms — not because coprolalia is actually more common, but because it's more *noticeable*, which is a totally different thing. ## Bottom Line I don't think your personal experience is a reliable way to estimate the true population rate here — not because your observations are dishonest or made up, but because of exactly how visibility and disclosure work with this specific symptom. The studies, even with their fuzziness, are actually better positioned to catch the full range (including undiagnosed-in-daily-life mild cases through clinical assessment) than personal anecdote, which almost mechanically filters toward the dramatic cases.
